IIMMAAGGEESS
       ppaammttoowwiinniiccoonn reads a (multi-image) Netpbm PAM file as input and outputs
       an single Windows icon file containing those images.

       The images in the icon file are in the same order as in the PAM input.



   PPAAMM IInnppuutt
       ppaammttoowwiinniiccoonn interprets the PAM images as follows, based on the  number
       of channels.


       l   l.    _   channels  image   1    fully   opaque   grayscale   image
       2    grayscale image with transparency channel 3    fully opaque  color
       image  4    color image with transparency channel 5    color image with
       transparency channel and additional AND mask

       The tuple types of the PAMs are irrelevant.



   AANNDD MMaasskk
       The so-called "AND mask" is a  special  feature  of  Microsoft  Windows
       icons.  It is required for all BMP encoded images.  At the first sight,
       the AND mask is a 1-bit transparency channel, but it is also  used  for
       e.g.  shading  the  icon  while dragging.  See WWiinnddoowwss IIccoonnss(1) for de‐
       tails.

       If there is no explicit AND mask, but transparency data  in  the  input
       image, ppaammttoowwiinniiccoonn sets the AND mask to opaque where the sample in the
       transparency channel is below maxval, and to transparent elsewhere.

       If no transparency data is present in the input image, ppaammttoowwiinniiccoonn as‐
       sumes the whole image to be fully opaque.



   BBMMPP OOuuttppuutt
       When  BMP encoding an image, ppaammttoowwiinniiccoonn tries to use the most compact
       BBII__RRGGBB format allowed for icon images with a color depth of eight  bits
       per channel.

       ppaammttoowwiinniiccoonn  generates  neither 16-bit BBII__RRGGBB nor BBII__BBIITTFFIIEELLDDSS BMP en‐
       coded images, even if the maxval of the input PAM is not 255.



